
            // Ensures that the list still has the same number of elements after getting the item.
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

            // Ensures that the list contains the returned item.
            Assert.IsTrue(New_containsNewRet);

            // Ensures that the returned item is the element at the specified index.
            Assert.AreEqual((int)(arrList[Old_index]), New_Ret);
        }}}


A: Yes, @MarcinJuraszek was right.
It turns out that, in the ArrayList implementation, the [Index] getter does not actually take any specific steps to observe the array, so there were no mutations happening. 

In order to check what the values actually were, instead of observing the value you should have observed the whole ArrayList object, not just the element at a given index. 

You'd see a difference in the results if you changed your test code from:

New_Ret = (int) (arrList[index]);

to:

New_arrList = arrList;

Now, New_arrList is an ArrayList rather than just a single element, so the actual ArrayList object (including the elements) will be observed:

PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList", New_arrList);

This will let you see all the contents of the ArrayList, not just one element. 

So, indeed in this case, there was no interesting value to observe at the array index level, just some basic property checking. Instead, checking the entire ArrayList object for values would have shown the effect of your code. 

I hope this clarifies things for others reading the question. It seems that Pex was not smart enough to infer interesting mutations at the Array indexers, even though it is a valid operation to get an item from an array at a given index.

Thanks for pointing it out @MarcinJuraszek.
Q: How to make a Python class instance callable as if a function I've got a quite complex setup and I'm trying to make class methods directly callable as functions for debugging purposes, so they can be called directly in the console.
I've tried to use the types module but I can't get my head around it.
Here's the rough pseudocode of what I want to achieve:
class MyClass:
    def __init__(self):
        self.var = 'Hello, world!'

    def my_function(self):
        print(self.var)

    # More functions ...

x = MyClass()

# I want to make my function directly callable here like so: x.my_function()


A: You can subclass the MyClass class and override the __call__ method in the subclass.  I've added your own methods to this example to show you how you can add multiple helper methods with the same structure:
class MyClass:
    def __init__(self):
        self.var = 'Hello, world!'

    def my_function(self):
        print(self.var)

class MyClassCallable(MyClass):
    def __call__(self):
        return self.my_function()

    def another_function(self):
        print('Another function:', self.var)

    def yet_another_function(self):
        print('Yet another function:', self.var)

x = MyClassCallable()
x()  # Equivalent of x.my_function()
x.another_function()
x.yet_another_function()

The __call__ method in Python allows instances of a class to be treated as callable. This enables code like x() to call the my_function method of the instance x.
In this example, the MyClassCallable class is a subclass of MyClass that overrides the __call__ method, allowing instances of MyClassCallable to be treated as if they were functions. The functions my_function, another_function, and yet_another_function are all added to instances of MyClassCallable in the same way that you'd add methods to a class.

