First, make sure you have all the proper namespaces and imports to get full access to ArrayList's methods and properties. 

Secondly, in your test case method, you'll want to have the precondition that the index is within the bounds of the ArrayList. You do this through the PexAssumeUnderTest method where you call AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0), which is a condition that makes sure the index is within the ArrayList. 

Lastly, you'll want to retrieve the previous index, then after getting the value of the specific index, you can make sure that this new value is in the ArrayList's array. This can be done by using the ArrayList.Contains method. 

Notice that I used PexObserve methods for viewing values and results with PexLab for understanding how test works at the moment. You can remove this part if you don't use it. 

Also keep in mind that ArrayList is deprecated and shouldn't be used for new development. In general, List would be a better choice.
Regularly check the MSDN and .NET's official documentation for any updates regarding using ArrayList.
