A: Test cases like you provided could be useful for testing the Get method of ArrayList class. These tests should cover various test cases like what happens when you try to get a value from an empty ArrayList, from an ArrayList with one element, from an ArrayList with many elements, from a negative index, and from an index greater than the size of the ArrayList.

But keep in mind, ArrayList is essentially a wrapper around a basic array which can cause all kinds of issues or exceptions if you try to access indices that aren't within the bounds of your array. The ArrayList class implements IList interface which uses the Count property to determine the size of the collection and provides a way to access all items by indices.

You can use System.IndexOutOfRangeException exception for checking the negative and greater than size index cases.
Therefore, you can use [PexMethod] above your test to Pex the method with all permutations as per the assumptions.

As far as the 'PUT_GetContract' method name is concerned, it is quite standard in testing context, but it doesn't have to be an exact match with the actual method name. It's up to your organization's naming convention or coding standard.

Note: I would suggest to use List<T> instead of ArrayList, as later is considered obsolete and not in use. List<T> is more flexible and provides all the features that ArrayList provides. 

A: You can create a test case as follows:
public void TestGet(ArrayList list, int index)
{ 
   // Assume the list is not null
   Assume.True(list != null);

   // Assume that the index is between 0 and the list count 
   Assume.True(index >=0 && index < list.Count);

   // Call the method
   object ret = list[index]; 

   // Check the result
   Assert.That(ret, Is.EqualTo(list[index]));
}

This test case checks that you can retrieve an object at a specific index from an ArrayList object. It assumes that the list is not null and that the index is within the bounds of the ArrayList and returns the object at that index.
It then checks whether the retrieved object and the object from the ArrayList at the same index are the same.
Remember to import PexLibraries and use proper namespaces including System.Collections, NUnit.Framework and Microsoft.Pex.Framework.
Also take note that this example does not fully cover the use of PEX, this is just a very basic example. Usually we will define multiple assumptions and check a multitude of postconditions for fully testing a method.
ArrayList is mostly obsolete and you are advised to use List<T>, Stack<T>, Queue<T> etc. as their functionalities are more extensive and tested by Microsoft. 
