A: Here is your issue. ArrayList is derived from the System.Collections.ArrayList class. However, in the .NET Parameterized unit testing framework, the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute means you should run the tests for all the methods of the target class and for these methods only. It doesn't mean run the tests over the class where the target class is derived.
Instead, you can use [PexAssemblyUnderTest] attribute for your Test class then only testing for the given class will be performed. Remember to add the PexAssemblyUnderTest attribute at the top of your Test Class. This attribute is going to specify the assembly to which Pex will apply its analysis.

A: 
I understand that [PexMethod] and [PexAssemblyUnderTest] act differently, [PexMethod] operates only on a single method, whereas [PexAssemblyUnderTest] operates on an entire assembly. What puzzles me is that Pex is not taking the [PexAssemblyUnderTest] into effect and is still performing testing against all the methods in the ArrayList class.

Yes, you're on the right track. [PexAssemblyUnderTest] indeed marks the assembly under test for a parameterized test run. 
However, keep in mind that the attribute should be used on the class where you're defining the tests, not on the derived classes. Here, you'd use it on your TestClass:

[PexClass(typeof(YourTestClass))]
[PexAssemblyUnderTest("YourTestClassAssembly")]
public partial class YourTestClass
{
    ...
}

Another thing to check might be how your code is properly referencing the tests library and project references. Make sure that the project that contains the tests references all necessary project references assemblies, and the tests library is included in the test settings.

Moreover, ensure that you have the necessary Pex assemblies in your references. If you've just copied the examples and didn't include the necessary Pex dlls, this might also explain why you're seeing such behavior. 

When the code was instrumented (by MS Pex), there are some lines in the output showing the method it chose to instrument. If you can see your method being picked up by Pex, that would be a good sign Pex is functioning correctly.

Hope this clarifies things a bit for you !!

