Regarding your question, Pex might not provide good or efficient test cases if the data it works on cannot be fully specified in the test method's signature.
Pex operates by creating new combinations of inputs for a method under test. When it encounters something it doesn't understand during code generation (like a generic or a complex type), it just skips it and moves on to the next input. 
