A: Firstly, you should be aware that the Pex-contracts are just for covering more brute-force cases, they're still going to be subject to other edge-cases you didn't test, as stated by the MSDN:

The Pex method will generate at most 2n distinct test cases (where n is the number of input parameters) plus one for each execution path of the method.

Since there's a fixed limit to the number of tests Pex can generate, it's advised to focus on complex and meaningful edge cases when using Pex to test your code.
Regarding the usage of Debug.Assert, yes, it's a strong tool to verify your code is working as expected, but it can be tricky to use with unit tests. If the Assert is failing when PEX is running your test, this doesn't mean the original code has a logical issue, it could be because PEX generated a test that triggers your Assert. 
In this case, when using PEX you should not rely on Assert or any other tool for code confirmations. Instead, use PEX's asserts or use PEX's outputs to validate your code's logic.
I would recommend that PEX is used more as an aid / utility rather than a literal assertion tool. It is quite possible that something isn't behaving as expected, simply because PEX is running an unexpected test case.
Note: For the second point, the Assert calls are normally only made when you have a specific reason to believe there could be an issue. Pex may create a test case that calls these Assert calls, but it doesn't mean that your original code has a problem.

A: Just to add some details:


*

*If you run Pex unit test on any class which contains Debug.Assert in its methods, the test cases that cause the Debug.Assert to be hit by Pex test are essentially test cases that you have not considered in your unit test. So those classes, methods, or even tests will not add value to your unit test coverage. They are often irrelevant, and are causing unnecessary failures.

*Moreover, the reason we have Debug.Assert in our code to help debugging, they don't get covered by Pex unit tests. Therefore, they don't add value to the coverage report.

*Instead of using Debug.Assert, consider using code contract assertions. Using code contract assertions, you can have Pex run tests on your code contract checks, which would help you to validate the public contract of your code. 


A: To complement on the valuable discussion on Pex and Assert usage:
The Pex method will generate 2n test cases (where n is the number of input parameters) plus one for each execution path of the method. The generated testing should be enough for identifying boundary and common cases, but remember that Pex will not generate a thorough suite covering the entire spectrum of your program behavior or error branches. You need to consider to add additional unit test for your tests to verify the program meets your expectations in other scenarios or at the edge cases.
Pex method will not generate tests for cases that are handled by Debug.Assert in the production code. And the Assert would not be the best place to make production level verification. So when using Pex, it should only be used for adding coverage to the areas that are not yet covererd by manual or auto-generated tests.
About the test failues, it may happen if the Pex generated a test scenario that triggers the Debug.Assert. The Assert calls are normally only made when you have a specific reason to believe there could be an issue. Pex may create a test case that calls these Assert calls, but it doesn’t mean that your original code has a problem.
Debug.Assert should be handled in your unit tests. If using Pex and you got a test fail that you think means your code have a problem, run the test in debug mode and analyze the situation yourself to identify the issue if there is one.
In summary, Pex is a tool that helps to cover more test cases and to understand the non-obvious behavior of the application, but it doesn't cover every possible scenario that could break the code. You need to have unit tests covering these edge cases and failure branches to ensure reliable code.
Thanks for the valuable discussion to consider--------------------              
            Debug.Assert((New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount) && (New_containsNewRet));
        }
    }
}

