A: I suspect that it is not feasible to write an arbitrary test case to test ArrayList using Pex. You may try using Pex with other types of classes, for example, you may try testing your own customized class instead of ArrayList. Another thing you could do is to create a test framework yourself based on Pex. 
Pex is a great tool for automatically generating test cases, but it has its limitations. When it comes to complex, stateful objects like ArrayList, it can generate tests that may not be meaningful or correct. 
For example, let's say you add an item to ArrayList then attempt to retrieve it later. Pex might generate a test where you remove the item you just added. This seems to be redundant and unreal because you just tested adding, not removing. So you might have to come up with your own custom test cases.
The more complex, the object, the harder it is to test it thoroughly.
Hope this helps to some extent.
Q: jQuery - Using same event handler for multiple elements I was wondering if there's a way to assign one event handler to multiple elements, using jQuery without duplicating the code.
For instance:
$('#element1, #element2, #element3').click(function(){
  // do something
});

I would like to accomplish this without repeating the same code.

A: Yes, you can definitely do this.
What you're doing is assigning one event handler to multiple elements.
When you use multiple selectors in jQuery like this, you're actually assigning the same handler to all those elements.
Here's an example:
HTML:
<div id="element1">Element 1</div>
<div id="element2">Element 2</div>
<div id="element3">Element 3</div>

jQuery:
$('#element1, #element2, #element3').click(function(){
  alert('You clicked ' + $(this).attr('id'));
});

In the example above, a handler is assigned to the click event of three different elements. When you click any of those, an alert is shown which tells you which element you clicked.

A: In jQuery, it is very common and standard practice to use a class selector rather than id for multiple elements. It would look something like this:
.class1, .class2, .class3 {
    //CSS code here
}

And then your jQuery would be:
$('.class1, .class2, .class3').click(function(){
  // Do something
});

This is more efficient especially when dealing with multiple elements that share the same functionality.
Example IDs and Classes:
<div id="element1" class="class1">Some Div 1</div>
<div id="element2" class="class2">Some Div 2</div>
<div id="element3" class="class3">Some Div 3</div>

And your jQuery would be same as:
$('.class1, .class2, .class3').click(function(){
  // do something
});


A: You are already using a very standard way of doing this with jQuery using a selector to select multiple elements and then assigning the same click handler to those elements. This is the recommended way of handling this, also known as multiselector:     
$('#element1, #element2, #element3').click(function(){
  // do something
});

Please note that you can replace the IDs with classes, if you want to attach the same listener to multiple elements share the same class. You can simply change the argument to be a class selector.
Just to add, If you are going to add event listener to the same elements over and over, it would be a sign that your code can be optimized. The reason being that the event listener could potentially have a lot more work going on in it, rather--------------------	    // after a GET operation at "index"

        }
        
        private void AddElementsToArray(ArrayList arrList)
        {
       	 arrList.Add(14);
       	 arrList.Add(4);
       	 arrList.Add(8);
       	 arrList.Add(44);
       	 arrList.Add(4000);
       	 arrList.Add(1);
       	 arrList.Add(17);
       	 arrList.Add(113);
        }

    }

}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListCustomizationTest.cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Helpers;
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListCustomizationTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void TestCustomizedCollection(PexAssume ensure)
        {
            string result = null;

            // Construct the sample collection
            List<int> sampleCollection = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };

            // Create customization to only test odd numbers
            PexCustomization<IEnumerable<int>> customization =
            PexCustomization.Create<IEnumerable<int>>(sampleCollection.ToArray())
            .WithCustomizer(EnumerableCustomizer.Skip<int>(x => x % 2 == 0));
            
            try
            {
                foreach (var number in (IEnumerable<int>)PexSettings.Default.TargetTypes[0].GetConstructors()[0].Invoke(new object[] { customization.Value }))
                {
                    result = result + number.ToString();
                }
            }
            catch (System.Collections.Generic.List<int>)
            {                
                result = "ArrayList";
            }

            // Assert that the array list is not empty
            ensure.Assert(result != null, $"result must not be null");

        }        
    }
}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayListSortTest.cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Helpers;
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [PexClass(typeof(ArrayList))]
    public class ArrayListSortTest
    {
        private ArrayList GenerateRandomNonRepeatedList(int size)
        {
            int min = 1;
            int max = 1000;
            bool isNotRepeat = true;

            ArrayList nonRepeated = new ArrayList();

            Random _r = new Random();
            int i = 0;

            while (i < size)
            {
                int num = _r.Next(min, max);
                foreach (int n in nonRepeated)
                {
                    if (n == num)
                    {
                        isNotRepeat = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (isNotRepeat)
                {
                    nonRepeated.Add(num);
                    i++;
                }

                isNotRepeat = true;
            }

            return nonRepeated;
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void Sort(ArrayList list)
        {
            ArrayList sortedList = new ArrayList(list);
            sortedList.Sort();

            bool isSorted = true;
            for (int i = 0; i < sortedList.Count - 1; i++)
            {
                if ((int)sortedList[i] > (int)sortedList[i + 1])
                {
                    isSorted = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            //Assertion 
            PexAssert.IsTrue(isSorted);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing(sortedList);
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void Sort_RandomNumbers(int Count, PexAssume PexAssumption)
        {
            ArrayList collection = GenerateRandomNonRepeatedList(Count);
            Sort(collection);
        }
    }
}

